    "lawCode": "Amd §33.13, Ment Hyg L",
    "lawSection": "Mental Hygiene Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S5680\n\nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the mental hygiene law, in relation to clinical\nrecords and confidentiality\n\nPURPOSE:\n\nTo provide access to clinical mental health records to the agency\nproviding protection and advocacy services to disabled New Yorkers\npursuant to federal law, consistent with Executive Law 558(b).\n\nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 amends § 33.13 (c) (4) of the mental hygiene law.\n\nSection 2 adds a new subdivision 17 to § 3113 (c) of the mental\nhygiene law. Section 3 provides for an effective date.\n\nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nOn June 30, 2013, the Justice Center for the Protection of People with\nSpecial Needs was created and assumed many of the oversight\nresponsibilities of the former Commission on Quality of Care and\nAdvocacy for Persons With Disabilities (CQCAPD). Under federal\nmandate, on June 1, 2013, the Governor re-designated the federally\nauthorized and funded protection and advocacy (P&A) system and client\nassistance program (CAP) as an independent agency providing federal\noversight of the state's system of care for individuals with\ndisabilities. Prior to June 1, 2013, the CQCAPD contracted with\nvarious regional non-profit agencies which provided protection and\nadvocacy services for people with disabilities pursuant to federal\nlaw.\n\nAt the time the legislation authorizing the creation of the Justice\nCenter and the carve-out of the independent federal oversight agency\nwas enacted, the legislature failed to amend Mental Hygiene Law\n33.13 (c) (4) to provide for access to records from the Office of\nMental Health and the Office for People with Developmental\nDisabilities to the Justice Center or to the independent federal\noversight agency. Prior to the 2013 legislation, both the CQCAPD and\nthe non-profit agencies providing protection and advocacy services\nwere entitled to records under statue.\n\nThis bill allows the Justice Center's access to such clinical records,\nand adds a separate provision permitting access for the\nnow-independent federal P&A and CAP system. These state and federal\nsystems, in carrying out their authorized oversight and advocacy\nfunctions, are distinct from \"qualified persons\" who are provided\naccess to records pursuant to section 33.16 and for whom copying fees\nmay be imposed pursuant to that section.\n\nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nThis is a new bill.\n\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nMinimal.\n\nLOCAL FISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\n\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n\n",
